FINAL ANSWER:
Status: I ran `git blame` on every tracked `.go` file at HEAD and counted surviving lines per author. Here are the results.

I excluded `vendor/`, which is 802 lines of third-party `ansi.go` added by Priya Raman. That leaves 14,511 lines. I used `-w -M -C` so whitespace edits and moved lines don't change authorship.

| # | Author | Surviving lines | Share |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Eric Paris | 2,194 | ~15% |
| 2 | Noah Dietz | 1,240 | ~8.5% |
| 3 | Alex Ogier | 1,214 | ~8.4% |
| 4 | Jonathan Reyna | 1,062 | ~7.3% |
| 5 | Marcus Lindqvist | 956 | ~6.6% |

Next are Tamal Saha (~4%), Riobard (~3.8%) and Mitch Connors (~3.4%). I merged Mitch's two identities, `Mitch Connors` and `therealmitchconnors`.

Caveats before you pick anyone:
- **Line count doesn't match commit activity.** Noah Dietz (2 commits, 2018) and Jonathan Reyna (1 commit, 2017) wrote the generated-style slice flag types (`BoolSlice`, `UintSlice`, `Int32Slice` and similar). Those are large but repetitive. Neither has committed since.
- **Marcus Lindqvist's lines are 2 commits from yesterday** (2026-10-21), the shell completion helpers and their tests. They show up under two emails (`<email redacted>` and `mlindqvist@corp.example`). Check that both are the same person.
- **The commit-count leaders rank lower by lines.** Tomas Aschan (63 commits), Albert Nigmatzianov (24 + 6 as `bogem`) and Sebastiaan van Stijn (13) don't make the top five. They may be better candidates if you want people who maintain the project over time.
- **There is no `.mailmap`.** Other authors may also appear under more than one name. For example, `Albert Nigmatzianov` and `bogem` share an email, but I haven't merged them.

I can rerun this with time-windowed activity, such as commits in the last 2 years, or with tests and non-test code counted separately.

